some care homes were struggling  financially even before the outbreak of  coronavirus but Sky News can reveal a  group of care homes in the northeast of  England have taken the unprecedented  step of legally warning the council if  they don't take action to help them  financially within five days the market  will start to collapse we've obtained a  leaked copy of a letter from 21 care  homes to North Tyneside Council  declaring force majeure or a major  incident the letter says the care home  market within North Tyneside is facing  imminent collapse due to the council's  conduct in the lead-up to and during the  current kovat 19 pandemic it goes on the  council has failed to provide sufficient  and timely financial support to care  home providers to meet the additional  costs they're incurring due to covert 19  as vacancies increased due to deaths  with fewer and fewer if any new  admissions the running of homes within  North Tyneside is becoming increasingly  unsustainable the largest representative  body for social care in the UK is Care  England I asked them to comment on the  letter we already knew that the care  sector was really struggling financially  to deal with this pandemic but how much  more serious is it to declare a force  majeure or major incident declaring this  force majeure is something that has  never really happened before so this  tells us that we are in the midst of  that's the worst situation we have seen  in modern times what would you say to  people who have relatives in care homes  and nursing homes in this country care  providers will be doing everything to  make sure that the care is as good as it  possibly can be and that people have  good lives though where they should  worry is whether or not these services  are sustainable for the long term  declaring force majeure essentially  frees both parties from their  obligations under the contract due to  extraordinary events in certain  specified circumstances those  circumstances would normally include  such things as war riots acts of  terrorism earthquake or flood or  whatever  in a statement North Tyneside Council  said we're working hard together to keep  staff and residents safe occupancy  levels have been affected by the  pandemic and we're aware levels have  fallen below where they were ordinarily  expected to be where this has happened  we're working with those providers  directly in line with our statutory duty  it's not just the northeast of England  where anger is growing about the costs  of combating coronavirus in care homes  scams  large groups of we were thankfully ahead  of the curve so we had the sufficient to  keep us rolling but then we were paying  exorbitant prices for maths in clubs  some care homes say they feel they've  been pushed to take drastic action and  they're still fighting the devastating  impact of coronavirus Lisa Holland Sky  News
 

